<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uruk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uruk was one of the oldest and largest cities of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and is associated with the early development of writing and urban life. It was the city of the legendary king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>. Uruk features in the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, and it remained an important religious centre in Babylonian times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uruk.jpg" alt="Uruk" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Picture taken by Marcus Cyron · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Part_of_front_of_Inanna_temple_of_Kara_Indasch_from_Uruk_Vorderasiatisches_Museum_Berlin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Uruk, the archaeological site known today as Warka, lies in Muthanna Governorate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, east of the present bed of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, on an ancient channel of the river that is now dry. It is 93 kilometres northwest of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and 108 kilometres southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>.</p><p>The city formed when two smaller settlements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a>, Unug and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullaba">Kullaba</a>, developed into cities and later merged. Their temple complexes became the Eanna District, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a>, and the Anu District. A canal system, described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in the desert, ran through the city and joined it to the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and to the surrounding farmland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a>, or Uruk expansion, saw a shift from small agricultural villages to a larger urban centre with a full-time bureaucracy, military and stratified society. Uruk was significantly larger and more complex than the other settlements of its time, which were generally about 10 hectares. Sumerian traders and colonists exported its culture, though Uruk could not keep long-distance control over colonies such as Tell Brak by military force.</p><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> the city was founded by the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> ruled it in the 27th century BCE. Proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets found there include the oldest known writing to feature a personal name, that of Kushim, who as an accountant recorded transactions in barley. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> annexed Uruk and it declined, but it revived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, when the Eanna District was restored.</p><p>Following the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> it declined steeply until about 850 BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> annexed it as a provincial capital. It flourished under the Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, and the Bīt Reš temple was one of the two main centres of Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy</a>. A shift in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salinisation">salinisation</a> from irrigation may have contributed to its decline. By 300 CE it was mostly abandoned, and by about 700 CE completely so. The last dated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablet from Uruk, an astronomical almanac, dates to 79 or 80 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site covers about 5.5 square kilometres and has three major tells, those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eanna">Eanna</a> district, Bit Resh and Irigal. William Kennett Loftus visited it in 1849 and led the first excavations from 1850 to 1854. Julius Jordan's team from the German Oriental Society found the temple of Ishtar in 1912 and 1913, along with part of a brick city wall about 9 kilometres long, which is estimated to date from around 3000 BCE.</p><p>German archaeologists worked there for 39 seasons. Among the finds is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mask-of-warka">Mask of Warka</a>, a carved marble female face of about 3100 BCE, probably a depiction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a>. Linguists have also proposed that the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> derives from Uruk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uruk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He supplanted Ur-Zababa, founded the city of Akkad as his capital (a city whose precise location has never been identified archaeologically) and then defeated Lugal-zage-si of <b>Uruk</b>, who had unified much of Sumer and was the strongest opponent in the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hammurabi's conquest of Larsa in 1763 BCE brought all of southern Mesopotamia, including the ancient cities of Ur, <b>Uruk</b>, Isin and the religious centre of Nippur, under his rule, and greatly expanded the kingdom's revenue base and its control of the richest farmland in the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaic tablets from <b>Uruk</b> of the late fourth millennium BCE already mention it, and Ur-Nanshe of Lagash (c. 2500 BCE) recorded that the ships of Dilmun brought him timber from foreign lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lagash lay north-west of the junction of the Euphrates and Tigris and east of <b>Uruk</b>, at modern Al-Hiba in Dhi Qar Governorate, Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Utu-hegal of <b>Uruk</b> expelled the Gutian rulers who had dominated parts of the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Early Dynastic Ur was itself conquered: Eannatum of Lagash in the 25th century BCE, Lugalkinishedudu of <b>Uruk</b> around 2400 BCE and Lugalzagesi of Umma in the 24th century all held the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li></ul></section>
